<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adda (river)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Adda River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adda">Adda</a> is a river of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, about 313 km long, which rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, flows through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valtellina">Valtellina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-como">Lake Como</a> and joins the Po near Cremona. From the fifteenth century it marked much of the frontier between the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and the mainland territories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lodi">Battle of Lodi</a> at its bridge in 1796.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adda-river.jpg" alt="Adda (river)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Giorces assumed (based on copyright claims). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adda7.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
